The code statements that lead to the bug are:
<text>
if (!new_password2) {
    kfree_sensitive(new_password);
    return -ENOMEM;
}
...
rc = smb3_sync_session_ctx_passwords(cifs_sb, ses);
if (rc) {
    mutex_unlock(&ses->session_mutex);
    return rc;  // missing kfree_sensitive(new_password) and kfree_sensitive(new_password2)
}
</text>
Reason:
When `smb3_sync_session_ctx_passwords()` fails, the function returns immediately without freeing the previously allocated `new_password` and `new_password2` buffers. This causes a memory leak. Additionally, since these buffers may contain sensitive password data, it also results in a potential information leak. The error path for `kstrdup` of `new_password2` already correctly frees `new_password`, but the later error path after acquiring the mutex does not.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
rc = smb3_sync_session_ctx_passwords(cifs_sb, ses);
if (rc) {
    mutex_unlock(&ses->session_mutex);
    kfree_sensitive(new_password);
    kfree_sensitive(new_password2);
    return rc;
}
</text>
Reason:
By adding `kfree_sensitive(new_password)` and `kfree_sensitive(new_password2)` before returning, the allocated password buffers are properly freed and erased (via `kfree_sensitive`) in the error case. This eliminates the memory leak and prevents sensitive information from lingering in memory.

